EXPERIMENT 1 — Cluster OpenMP
Aim: To execute a C program in parallel using OpenMP threads.
What it does: OpenMP divides work into threads that run concurrently on the CPU of one computer.
Step 1: Install GCC
sudo apt update
sudo apt install build-essential -y

Step 2: Create the C program
cat > openmp_demo.c <<'EOF'
#include <stdio.h>
#include <omp.h>
 
int main() {
    #pragma omp parallel
    {
        int id = omp_get_thread_num();
        int total = omp_get_num_threads();
        printf("Hello from thread %d of %d\n", id, total);
    }
    return 0;
}
EOF

Step 3: Compile and run
gcc -fopenmp openmp_demo.c -o openmp_demo
./openmp_demo

Step 4: Run with four threads
OMP_NUM_THREADS=4 ./openmp_demo

Result: Each thread prints its thread ID. The order of output can change.
EXPERIMENT 2 — Load Balancer Using HAProxy
Aim: To distribute client requests between two web servers.
What it does: HAProxy receives requests on port 8080 and forwards them to Server 1 or Server 2 using round-robin balancing.
Step 1: Install packages
sudo apt update
sudo apt install haproxy python3 -y

Step 2: Create two web pages
mkdir -p ~/lb-lab/server1 ~/lb-lab/server2
echo '<h1>Hello from SERVER 1</h1>' > ~/lb-lab/server1/index.html
echo '<h1>Hello from SERVER 2</h1>' > ~/lb-lab/server2/index.html

Step 3: Start the two web servers (use two terminals)
cd ~/lb-lab/server1
python3 -m http.server 8001 --bind 127.0.0.1

In Terminal 2:
cd ~/lb-lab/server2
python3 -m http.server 8002 --bind 127.0.0.1

Step 4: Configure HAProxy
sudo nano /etc/haproxy/haproxy.cfg

Add/replace the configuration below (back up the existing file first if it contains other settings):
global
    daemon
defaults
    mode http
    timeout connect 5s
    timeout client 30s
    timeout server 30s
frontend web_frontend
    bind *:8080
    default_backend web_servers
backend web_servers
    balance roundrobin
    server server1 127.0.0.1:8001 check
    server server2 127.0.0.1:8002 check

Step 5: Check and start HAProxy
sudo haproxy -c -f /etc/haproxy/haproxy.cfg
sudo systemctl enable --now haproxy
sudo systemctl restart haproxy

Step 6: Test load balancing
for i in {1..6}; do curl -s http://127.0.0.1:8080; done

Result: Responses should alternate between the two server pages. Stop the Python servers with Ctrl+C.
EXPERIMENT 3 — Docker-Based Application Deployment
Aim: To package and run a simple HTML application inside a Docker container.
What it does: Docker builds an image from the HTML file and Dockerfile, then runs it as a container.
Step 1: Install Docker
sudo apt update
sudo apt install docker.io -y
sudo systemctl enable --now docker

Step 2: Create the application
mkdir docker-lab
cd docker-lab
cat > index.html <<'EOF'
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head><title>Docker Lab</title></head>
<body>
  <h1>Hello from Docker!</h1>
  <p>My first containerized web application.</p>
</body>
</html>
EOF

Step 3: Create the Dockerfile
cat > Dockerfile <<'EOF'
FROM nginx:alpine
COPY index.html /usr/share/nginx/html/index.html
EXPOSE 80
EOF

Step 4: Build the image
sudo docker build -t docker-lab:v1 .
sudo docker images

Step 5: Run the container
sudo docker run -d --name web-container -p 8080:80 docker-lab:v1
sudo docker ps

Step 6: Test the application
curl http://localhost:8080

Step 7: Stop and remove the container
sudo docker stop web-container
sudo docker rm web-container

Result: The HTML page is served through http://localhost:8080.
EXPERIMENT 4 — KVM Virtual Machine Migration
Aim: To migrate a virtual machine from one KVM host to another.
What it does: A running VM is transferred from a source host to a destination host. This requires two compatible Linux hosts; a simple file copy is not VM migration.
Step 1: Install KVM/libvirt on BOTH hosts
sudo apt update
sudo apt install qemu-kvm libvirt-daemon-system libvirt-clients virt-manager -y
sudo systemctl enable --now libvirtd

Step 2: Check KVM and libvirt on both hosts
sudo apt install cpu-checker -y
kvm-ok
virsh version
virsh list --all

Step 3: Check network connectivity
On the source host, find its IP and test the destination host:
ip -br addr
ping -c 4 DESTINATION_HOST_IP

Step 4: Check the VM on the source host
virsh list --all
virsh dominfo VM_NAME
virsh domblklist VM_NAME

The VM must be running for live migration. Both hosts need compatible CPU settings, network configuration and access to the VM disk (commonly shared storage).
Step 5: Run live migration from the source host
sudo virsh migrate --live --persistent --undefinesource VM_NAME qemu+ssh://DESTINATION_USER@DESTINATION_HOST_IP/system

Step 6: Verify on the destination host
virsh list --all

Result: The VM should be running on the destination host and no longer running on the source. The migration command will not work until SSH/libvirt access and storage are configured.
EXPERIMENT 5 — Eavesdropping Using TCPdump
Aim: To capture and view packets travelling through a network interface.
What it does: tcpdump displays packet information such as source IP, destination IP and protocol.
Step 1: Install tcpdump
sudo apt update
sudo apt install tcpdump -y

Step 2: Find the network interface
ip -br addr
sudo tcpdump -D

Note the active interface name, for example enp0s3 or eth0.
Step 3: Capture packets
sudo tcpdump -i enp0s3

Stop with Ctrl+C. Replace enp0s3 with your interface.
Step 4: Capture only 10 packets
sudo tcpdump -i enp0s3 -c 10

Step 5: Filter packets
sudo tcpdump -i enp0s3 icmp
sudo tcpdump -i enp0s3 port 80
sudo tcpdump -i enp0s3 host 192.168.1.10

Step 6: Save and read packets
sudo tcpdump -i enp0s3 -w capture.pcap
sudo tcpdump -r capture.pcap

Result: Packet details are displayed or saved to capture.pcap. Capture only traffic you are authorised to inspect.
EXPERIMENT 6 — Google Colab-Based Data API Calling
Aim: To fetch data from a REST API using Python in Google Colab.
What it does: Python sends an HTTP GET request to an API, receives JSON data and displays selected fields.
Step 1: Open Google Colab
Go to https://colab.research.google.com and create a new notebook.
Step 2: Import requests and call the API
import requests
 
url = "https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/users"
response = requests.get(url, timeout=15)
 
print("Status code:", response.status_code)
data = response.json()
print(data[:3])

Step 3: Display selected data
for user in data:
    print("Name:", user["name"])
    print("Email:", user["email"])
    print("---")

Step 4: Check request errors
response.raise_for_status()
print("Records received:", len(data))

Result: User data is received in JSON format and names/emails are printed. HTTP status 200 means the request succeeded.
EXPERIMENT 7 — Xen-Based Virtual Machine Migration
Aim: To migrate a Xen virtual machine from one Xen host to another.
What it does: The Xen toolstack transfers a running VM to another Xen host. Two Xen-enabled hosts and prepared VM storage/networking are required.
Step 1: Check Xen on both hosts
xl info
xl list

Step 2: Check destination connectivity
hostname
ip -br addr
ping -c 4 DESTINATION_HOST_IP

Step 3: Identify the running VM on the source host
sudo xl list

Step 4: Migrate the VM from the source host
sudo xl migrate VM_NAME DESTINATION_HOST

Replace VM_NAME and DESTINATION_HOST with the actual VM name and destination host. SSH access and destination storage/network configuration must already be set up.
Step 5: Verify migration
Run the following on the destination host, then on the source host:
sudo xl list

Result: The VM should appear on the destination host and no longer appear as running on the source host.
EXPERIMENT 8 — Hosting an Application Using KVM
Aim: To host a web page inside a KVM virtual machine and access it from the host.
What it does: KVM runs an Ubuntu guest VM; Nginx inside that VM serves the HTML page over HTTP.
Step 1: Install KVM
sudo apt update
sudo apt install qemu-kvm libvirt-daemon-system libvirt-clients virt-manager -y
sudo systemctl enable --now libvirtd

Step 2: Create an Ubuntu virtual machine
virt-manager

In Virtual Machine Manager: create a new VM → select the Ubuntu ISO → allocate RAM, CPU and disk → install Ubuntu → start the VM.
Step 3: Install Nginx INSIDE the VM
sudo apt update
sudo apt install nginx -y
sudo systemctl enable --now nginx

Step 4: Create the web page INSIDE the VM
echo "<h1>Hello from an application hosted on KVM!</h1>" | sudo tee /var/www/html/index.html

Step 5: Find the VM IP and test from the host
hostname -I
curl http://VM_IP_ADDRESS

Result: The page is served by Nginx inside the VM. With NAT networking it is usually reachable from the host; LAN access may require bridged networking.
ADDITIONAL EXPERIMENT — Master–Slave Cluster Configuration
Aim: To connect one master machine to two slave machines and exchange messages over the network.
What it does: The master connects to slave nodes using SSH and sends a message to a Python TCP server running on each slave.
Step 1: Configure host names on ALL machines
sudo nano /etc/hosts

Add the same host mapping on each machine, changing IPs to match your lab:
192.168.1.10 master
192.168.1.11 slave1
192.168.1.12 slave2

Step 2: Set each machine's hostname
Run the matching command on each PC:
sudo hostnamectl set-hostname master
# On slave 1: sudo hostnamectl set-hostname slave1
# On slave 2: sudo hostnamectl set-hostname slave2
hostname

Step 3: Check IP, CPU and connectivity
ip -br addr
ip route
nproc
lscpu
ping -c 4 192.168.1.11
ping -c 4 192.168.1.12

Step 4: Install and start SSH on BOTH slaves
sudo apt update
sudo apt install openssh-server python3 -y
sudo systemctl enable --now ssh
sudo systemctl is-active ssh
whoami
hostname -I

Step 5: Set up SSH key on the master
sudo apt update
sudo apt install openssh-client python3 -y
ssh-keygen -t ed25519
ssh-copy-id SLAVE_USERNAME@192.168.1.11
ssh-copy-id SLAVE_USERNAME@192.168.1.12

Test passwordless login:
ssh SLAVE_USERNAME@192.168.1.11 hostname

Step 6: Create and run the TCP server on EACH slave
cat > slave_hello.py <<'PY'
import socket
 
HOST = "0.0.0.0"
PORT = 5000
 
with socket.socket(socket.AF_INET, socket.SOCK_STREAM) as server:
    server.setsockopt(socket.SOL_SOCKET, socket.SO_REUSEADDR, 1)
    server.bind((HOST, PORT))
    server.listen()
    print("Slave listening on port", PORT)
 
    while True:
        conn, addr = server.accept()
        with conn:
            message = conn.recv(1024).decode()
            print("Received from Master:", message)
            conn.sendall(b"Hello received successfully")
PY
python3 slave_hello.py

Keep this program running on both slaves. Open a separate terminal on each slave if needed.
Step 7: Create and run the client on the MASTER
cat > master_hello.py <<'PY'
import socket
 
SLAVES = ["192.168.1.11", "192.168.1.12"]
PORT = 5000
 
for ip in SLAVES:
    try:
        with socket.create_connection((ip, PORT), timeout=5) as client:
            client.sendall(b"Hello from Master")
            print(ip, client.recv(1024).decode())
    except OSError as error:
        print(ip, "ERROR:", error)
PY
python3 master_hello.py

Result: The master receives “Hello received successfully” from each slave. If the connection fails, check IP addresses, SSH/server status and firewall rules.

 Quick Notes for PoE
• KVM migration and Xen migration need two properly configured hosts; one PC alone is not enough for real host-to-host migration.
• For HAProxy, keep both Python servers running while testing the load balancer.
• For Docker, run the build command from the folder containing both index.html and Dockerfile.
• For TCPdump, use the actual active interface name shown by ip -br addr.
• For the Colab API experiment, run each code cell from top to bottom.
• Replace all sample IPs, usernames, interface names and VM names with your lab values.
